course: dictionary
lesson: 2229
title: Covet: wanting what is not yours
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To covet something is to want it very much, especially when it belongs to someone else. Covet is a verb, a doing word, and its endings change: covets, coveted, coveting. The clue is in the sentence «do not covet what is theirs», which means do not want what belongs to other people.
explain: Covet is stronger than just liking something. If you like a bike, that is fine. If you badly want your friend's bike and feel cross that it is not yours, that is coveting. The word shows a long, hungry wanting.
explain: The word often appears in a moral or religious setting. In the Bible, «You shall not covet» is the last of the Ten Commandments, the rules about treating others kindly. It asks people to be content, which means happy with what they have.
fact: Covet means to want very much something that belongs to someone else.
fact: «Covet» is a verb: covets, coveted, coveting.
fact: «You shall not covet» is the last of the Ten Commandments.
fact: A «coveted prize» is one that many people want.
fact: Useful phrases: «covet your neighbour's», «a coveted place».
exercise: «Do not covet what is theirs.» What does covet mean here? | To count and check things carefully | To give something away happily | To clean something very well | *To want something that belongs to someone else
exercise: In «a coveted prize», what does coveted mean? | Thrown away by everyone | Very small and light | Made of wood | *One that many people want
exercise: Yesterday Sam ___ his sister's puppy and wished it were his. Which word fits? | covet | covets | *coveted | coveting
test: Which sentence means the same as covet? | *To long for what belongs to someone else | To share your snacks with a friend | To say sorry quickly after a fall
test: A «coveted place» in the team means | *A place many people want | A place far, far away | A place nobody wants | A place with no seats
exam: What is the main idea of covet? | Giving away your favourite things | *Wanting very much what belongs to someone else | Counting coins carefully | Sleeping in a tent
